This project contains only code of our own (MIT, see LICENSE).

At runtime it drives, without patching or replacing any of them:
  - nftables (GPL-2.0) - the ruleset is generated and handed to "nft -f";
    the table is our own and no other table is touched
  - systemd (LGPL-2.1+), to enable nftables.service and read its state
  - procps-ng sysctl (GPL-2.0+), to load /etc/sysctl.d and read values back
  - kmod modprobe (LGPL-2.1+), asked what it WOULD do; nothing is loaded
  - iproute2 ss and ip (GPL-2.0+), read-only, for the listening sockets and
    the address of the wireless interface
  - polkit (LGPL-2.0+), which carries the action that lets the app's
    switches act without a password for the session at the device

It writes, and puts every one of them back on revert:
  - /etc/sysctl.d/99-furios-hardening.conf
  - /etc/modprobe.d/99-furios-hardening.conf
  - /etc/furios-security/ (the generated ruleset, the config, the state and
    a copy of whatever /etc/nftables.conf was before)
  - /etc/nftables.conf, which nftables.service reads and which therefore
    cannot be anywhere else

It reads, without changing them:
  - /proc/sys/... for the current value of every sysctl it sets
  - /proc/sys/kernel/osrelease, to know which kernel this is

No part of FuriOS is patched or replaced, and no file belonging to anybody
else is deleted: a file at one of our paths that does not carry our marker is
reported and left where it is.
